// Exercises the part ticket 20 put in the algebra: integer intervals.
//
// Ticket 08's rule is that the checker credits any condition it can translate
// into a type operation, and names `n > 1` as an interval refinement. Neither
// clause below is unguarded, so this function is exhaustive ONLY if the checker
// can see that `n <= 1` and `n > 1` partition `int`. Without intervals it would
// be rejected, and the author would have to weaken the last clause.
module Math

public int Fib(int n)

Fib(n) when n <= 1 -> n
Fib(n) when n > 1  -> Fib(n - 1) + Fib(n - 2)

// A closed partition with a hole in the middle, to prove the residual is exact
// rather than merely non-empty.
type Band = :low | :mid | :high

public Band Classify(int n)

Classify(n) when n < 10             -> :low
Classify(n) when n >= 10 and n < 100 -> :mid
Classify(n) when n >= 100           -> :high

// A destructuring bind, which is legal here for one reason: it CANNOT FAIL.
// `range` is declared `(int, int)`, so subtracting what the pattern matches
// leaves nothing, and the compiler proves that before emitting. Widen the
// parameter to `(int, int) | atom` and this same line is an error carrying the
// residual — irrefutability is checked, not assumed.
//
// `bool` is a type like any other: `:true | :false`, two atoms, no primitive.
public bool InBand(int n, (int, int) range)

InBand(n, range) ->
    var (lo, hi) = range
    n >= lo and n <= hi

// F8 and ticket 45 — `== name` matches the value a name ALREADY HOLDS, which is
// the one thing C# patterns cannot express at all (they push you to
// `when v == expected`). A bare name INTRODUCES; the marker is what asks for the
// match, and it reads as the equality member of the relational family.
//
// The second clause tests `head` rather than rebinding it. Note what it costs:
// a matched name credits NOTHING to the certain set, because the compiler does
// not know the value — so the third clause is doing real exhaustiveness work
// rather than padding, and deleting it would be an error rather than a tidy-up.
//
// Lowered with no guard and no comparison: a bound variable repeated in an
// Erlang pattern IS an equality test, so the target does the whole job. B# needs
// a token here only because it forbids rebinding and so cannot use that rule
// directly.
public int RunLength(int head, list<int> xs)

RunLength(head, [])                -> 0
RunLength(head, [== head, ..rest]) -> 1 + RunLength(head, rest)
RunLength(head, [_, ..rest])       -> 0
